   1: -- REQ-001 D5.L1 â€” the tenant-isolation policy set, organisation-member branch.
   2: --
   3: -- THE FIRST POLICIES IN THIS REPOSITORY. Row-level security has been ON since the first migration
   4: -- with ZERO policies, on purpose: that denies everybody, which is the safe default and is NOT the
   5: -- requirement. AT-001.21 and AT-001.22 need the right tenant to READ and the wrong one to be denied,
   6: -- so this migration both GRANTS and DENIES.
   7: --
   8: -- ============================================================================================
   9: -- WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS FILE, AND WHERE IT LANDS
  10: -- ============================================================================================
  11: --
  12: -- `public.viewer_is_platform_admin()`, the assigned-volunteer policy on `public.projects` and the
  13: -- platform-admin policies are NOT here. They are the next migration's, and the rule that puts them
  14: -- there is: A SLICE DOES NOT SHIP A POLICY BRANCH IT DOES NOT TEST. Several permissive `select`
  15: -- policies on one table are OR'd, so the set splits cleanly by BRANCH rather than by table â€” the
  16: -- branch below is the one AT-001.21's own Data API control exercises, and the branches that admit a
  17: -- volunteer and an administrator are exercised by AT-001.23 and AT-001.40 in the slice that ships
  18: -- them. Until then a volunteer and an administrator are denied at the Data API because no policy
  19: -- admits them, which is exactly what this slice's denials assert.
  20: --
  21: -- NOTHING IS GRANTED TO `anon`. The public surface of this product is an edge function
  22: -- (`public-project-page`), never a table. AT-001.17's second arm asserts that
  23: -- `GET /rest/v1/org_memberships` with the publishable key still answers 401 `permission denied`, and
  24: -- it still does: `anon` receives no grant here.
  25: --
  26: -- ============================================================================================
  27: -- THIS MIGRATION REVERSES AN EXPLICIT REVOKE, AND NAMES IT
  28: -- ============================================================================================
  29: --
  30: -- `20260811130000_single_seat_org_and_single_developer_projects.sql` line 123 carries
  31: --
  32: --     revoke all on table public.projects from anon, authenticated, service_role;
  33: --
  34: -- and its comment gives the reason: no PostgREST route reached that table, and Supabase's ALTER
  35: -- DEFAULT PRIVILEGES had granted REFERENCES, TRIGGER and TRUNCATE to all three roles, which
  36: -- row-level security does not cover. A later migration overriding an earlier one is ordinary; a
  37: -- SILENT reversal of a deliberate revoke is not, so it is named here.
  38: --
  39: -- WHY THE GRANT IS NOW CORRECT. The revoke's stated premise was that nothing reaches the table
  40: -- through the Data API. This leaf changes that premise: AT-001.21 probes `public.projects` by
  41: -- identifier and AT-001.22 reads a project, so the table now has a client-facing read, and a read
  42: -- denied at the PRIVILEGE layer would mean row-level security never ran at all. What is granted back
  43: -- is `select` and nothing else â€” strictly narrower than the default privileges the revoke removed,
  44: -- so REFERENCES, TRIGGER and TRUNCATE stay revoked for all three roles.
  45: 
  46: /* ============================================================ the policy helper ================ */
  47: 
  48: -- WHY A DEFINER FUNCTION AND NOT AN INLINE SUBQUERY. A `select` policy ON `public.org_memberships`
  49: -- that read `public.org_memberships` would recurse into itself: evaluating the policy runs the
  50: -- subquery, which is a read of the same table, which evaluates the policy again. A `security definer`
  51: -- function runs as its owner, whose reads are not subject to row-level security, so the recursion is
  52: -- cut structurally rather than by a flag somebody sets.
  53: --
  54: -- IT ANSWERS ONLY ABOUT `auth.uid()`, which is what makes the remote-procedure exposure below safe:
  55: -- there is no argument by which a caller can ask about somebody else's standing. A caller learns
  56: -- whether IT is seated in an organisation, which it already knows.
  57: create function public.viewer_is_org_member(p_org_id uuid)
  58: returns boolean
  59: language sql
  60: stable
  61: security definer
  62: set search_path = ''
  63: as $$
  64:   select exists (
  65:     select 1
  66:       from public.org_memberships m
  67:      where m.org_id = p_org_id
  68:        and m.account_id = (select auth.uid())
  69:   );
  70: $$;
  71: 
  72: comment on function public.viewer_is_org_member(uuid) is
  73:   'True when the calling user holds a membership row in that organisation (REQ-001, AT-001.21). Answers only about auth.uid().';
  74: 
  75: -- THE REVOKE-THEN-GRANT POSTURE, AND THE SECOND HALF IS THE PART A COPY WOULD GET WRONG.
  76: --
  77: -- PostgreSQL grants EXECUTE on a new function to PUBLIC by default and every Data API role inherits
  78: -- it, so the revoke is what closes the default. That much is the posture every definer function in
  79: -- this tree already has.
  80: --
  81: -- THE GRANT TO `authenticated` IS MANDATORY HERE, and copying the existing service-role-only posture
  82: -- would BREAK the policies below. A policy expression is evaluated AS THE QUERYING ROLE, so the
  83: -- querying role needs EXECUTE on any function the policy calls; without this grant every read below
  84: -- would fail with a permission error on the function instead of answering.
  85: revoke execute on function public.viewer_is_org_member(uuid) from public;
  86: grant execute on function public.viewer_is_org_member(uuid) to authenticated, service_role;
  87: 
  88: /* ============================================================ the privileges ==================== */
  89: 
  90: -- WHY A GRANT COMES BEFORE A POLICY, measured rather than reasoned and recorded in the first
  91: -- migration's own comment: `[api] auto_expose_new_tables` is unset in `supabase/config.toml`, so a
  92: -- table is NOT reachable through the Data API roles without an explicit grant. Left ungranted, a
  93: -- client read fails at the PRIVILEGE layer and row-level security is never consulted â€” so "denied by
  94: -- row-level security" would be a claim about a mechanism that did not run, and AT-001.21's denial
  95: -- would be measuring the tree's emptiness.
  96: grant select on public.organizations to authenticated;
  97: grant select on public.org_memberships to authenticated;
  98: grant select on public.acknowledgments to authenticated;
  99: grant select on public.projects to authenticated;
 100: 
 101: -- THE SERVICE ROLE READS FOR THE THREE NEW EDGE FUNCTIONS, and it needs the privilege even though it
 102: -- bypasses row-level security: bypassing a policy is not holding a table privilege.
 103: -- `public.accounts` and `public.org_memberships` already carry this grant from earlier migrations â€”
 104: -- `organizations` and `projects` did not, and `organization-dashboard`, `project-workspace` and
 105: -- `public-project-page` read both.
 106: --
 107: -- STILL NO INSERT, UPDATE OR DELETE ANYWHERE. Every write in this schema goes through a SECURITY
 108: -- DEFINER function, which is what stops the service-role key writing past `complete_signup`'s own
 109: -- refusals. These two statements are reads and nothing else.
 110: grant select on public.organizations to service_role;
 111: grant select on public.projects to service_role;
 112: 
 113: /* ============================================================ the policies ====================== */
 114: 
 115: -- ONE `select` POLICY PER TABLE, EACH NAMING ITS OWN TENANT KEY. Every one of them is a claim a
 116: -- reader can check against the criterion: an account reads the organisation it is seated in, that
 117: -- organisation's seat, that organisation's projects, and its OWN acknowledgments.
 118: --
 119: -- NO `using (true)` ANYWHERE, and the conformance arm of the next slice refuses one. A policy that
 120: -- admits every row satisfies "the table carries a select policy" while exposing everything, which is
 121: -- the difference between isolation and the appearance of it.
 122: 
 123: create policy organizations_select_org_member
 124:   on public.organizations
 125:   for select
 126:   to authenticated
 127:   using (public.viewer_is_org_member(id));
 128: 
 129: comment on policy organizations_select_org_member on public.organizations is
 130:   'An organisation is readable by the accounts seated in it (REQ-001, AT-001.21).';
 131: 
 132: create policy org_memberships_select_org_member
 133:   on public.org_memberships
 134:   for select
 135:   to authenticated
 136:   using (public.viewer_is_org_member(org_id));
 137: 
 138: comment on policy org_memberships_select_org_member on public.org_memberships is
 139:   'An organisation''s seat is readable by the accounts seated in that organisation (REQ-001, AT-001.21).';
 140: 
 141: create policy projects_select_org_member
 142:   on public.projects
 143:   for select
 144:   to authenticated
 145:   using (public.viewer_is_org_member(org_id));
 146: 
 147: comment on policy projects_select_org_member on public.projects is
 148:   'A project is readable by the accounts seated in its owning organisation (REQ-001, AT-001.21).';
 149: 
 150: -- ACKNOWLEDGMENTS ARE KEYED ON THE ACCOUNT, NOT ON AN ORGANISATION, so this one policy does not call
 151: -- the helper. `public.acknowledgments` holds a PLATFORM-level record of one person accepting the ToS
 152: -- and the Platform Promise; it has no `org_id` and belongs to no organisation. "Your own
 153: -- acknowledgments and nobody else's" is both the honest tenant rule for this table and strictly
 154: -- tighter than an organisation-wide one â€” which is what AT-001.21 needs, since NGO B must not read
 155: -- NGO A's acknowledgment row.
 156: create policy acknowledgments_select_own_account
 157:   on public.acknowledgments
 158:   for select
 159:   to authenticated
 160:   using (account_id = (select auth.uid()));
 161: 
 162: comment on policy acknowledgments_select_own_account on public.acknowledgments is
 163:   'An acknowledgment is readable by the account that made it (REQ-001, AT-001.21).';
 164: 
 165: -- `public.accounts` GETS NO POLICY, and its absence is deliberate rather than forgotten. It carries
 166: -- `grant select, insert â€¦ to authenticated` from the first migration and row-level security with no
 167: -- policy, so it reaches NO row through a client key â€” which is the state the first migration chose
 168: -- and no criterion in this leaf asks to change. `public.volunteer_profiles` is unreachable a
 169: -- different way, by `revoke all`. The conformance arm of the next slice classifies both, and it needs
 170: -- both tests because a grant check alone would call `accounts` reachable.
 171: 
 172: -- PostgREST caches the schema. Without this, the first read of a freshly granted table can answer out
 173: -- of a stale cache rather than out of the catalog this migration just changed.
 174: notify pgrst, 'reload schema';

